# Local settings, for `npm run dev` and for the notes commands. Copy to
# .env.local, which is git-ignored:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Vite reads the VITE_* variable out of it when the dev server starts, and
# scripts/notes.mjs reads the rest — so this is the whole of local setup. A
# shell variable overrides anything written here, and `--bucket` overrides both.

# Where `npm run dev` sends /api, which is where the published notes come from:
# the bucket is private, so the backend reads it and the browser asks its own
# origin. The default is the compose stack — `cd docker/app && docker compose
# up` — so this only needs setting for a backend run some other way, e.g. flask
# straight on 8000. Without something behind /api, /notes is an empty shelf and
# the rest of the app is unaffected.
# VITE_API_PROXY=http://localhost:8080

# The account's R2 endpoint, which the notes commands publish and pull through,
# and the AWS profile holding the R2 token they do it with. Both ends write
# now — the backend publishes the sheets uploaded from the editor — so this
# token and the backend's are the same permission, Object Read & Write, and
# separate only so either can be rolled alone. Written here, the two together
# mean a bare `npm run notes:pull` works in any shell. See
# docs/notes-r2-storage.md for where both come from.
FRETBOOK_R2_ENDPOINT=https://0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
AWS_PROFILE=r2

# Which bucket those commands act on. Leave it out and they use
# fretbook-notes-dev, which is what local work wants; set it, or pass --bucket,
# to reach production on purpose.
# FRETBOOK_NOTES_BUCKET=fretbook-notes-prod
